ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV)

Document ID: ADS-006
Title: Canonical Event (CEV)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Event (CEV), the standardized representation of immutable engineering events within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Canonical Events communicate significant changes in engineering state while preserving semantics, provenance, governance, and traceability.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Event model
Event hierarchy
Event metadata
Event relationships
Event lifecycle
Event governance
Event quality
Event interoperability

This specification applies to all engineering events within Atlas.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Event (CEV) is a specialization of the Canonical Atlas Object representing an immutable occurrence that records a significant engineering fact or state transition.

A Canonical Event announces that something happened.

It SHALL NOT request, instruct, or mandate future actions.

4. Objectives

The Canonical Event SHALL:

Represent immutable engineering facts.
Notify interested participants.
Preserve event history.
Enable event-driven cognition.
Support traceability.
Support replay.
Support interoperability.
5. Inheritance

The Canonical Event SHALL inherit every mandatory property of the Canonical Atlas Object.

Canonical Atlas Object
        │
        ▼
 Canonical Event
        │
 ┌──────┼──────────────┐
 ▼      ▼              ▼
Observation   Workflow   Decision
Event         Event      Event

Future event families SHALL extend this hierarchy.

6. Canonical Event Types

Atlas defines the following event families.

Event Family	Description
Observation Event	New engineering observation
Evidence Event	Evidence validated
Knowledge Event	Knowledge updated
Decision Event	Decision created or changed
Workflow Event	Workflow transition
Governance Event	Policy or governance change
Trust Event	Security or trust change
Integration Event	External system interaction
Platform Event	Platform operational state
7. Mandatory Event Properties

Every Canonical Event SHALL define:

Property	Description
Event ID	Globally unique identifier
Event Type	Canonical event classification
Event Name	Human-readable name
Event Timestamp	Time the event occurred
Producer	Producing component
Subject	Entity affected
Payload	Canonical event content
Context	Engineering context
Correlation ID	Related activity identifier
Causation ID	Originating event identifier

These properties supplement the inherited CAO properties.

8. Event Immutability

After publication, a Canonical Event SHALL NOT be modified.

If corrections are required, a new event SHALL be published referencing the original event.

Historical events SHALL remain permanently available according to retention policies.

9. Event Relationships

Canonical Events SHALL support explicit relationships.

