Additional relationships MAY be introduced through future specifications.

10. Event Ordering

Atlas supports three ordering models.

Strict Ordering

Events processed sequentially.

Partition Ordering

Ordering preserved within a partition.

Unordered Processing

Ordering not guaranteed.

The applicable ordering model SHALL be declared by the producing service.

11. Event Lifecycle

A Canonical Event SHALL progress through the following lifecycle.

Detected
     │
Validated
     │
Published
     │
Distributed
     │
Consumed
     │
Archived

Lifecycle transitions SHALL be auditable.

12. Provenance

Every Canonical Event SHALL preserve:

Producing service
Producing workflow
Source object
Triggering event
Processing context
Engineering context
Correlation identifiers

Provenance SHALL be immutable.

13. Replay Model

Atlas SHALL support replay of Canonical Events.

Replay SHALL:

Preserve original timestamps.
Preserve original identifiers.
Preserve ordering semantics.
Preserve provenance.

Replay SHALL NOT modify historical events.

14. Quality Requirements

Every Canonical Event SHALL satisfy:

Accuracy
Completeness
Integrity
Traceability
Immutability
Replayability
Interoperability
Provenance integrity
15. Governance

Every Canonical Event SHALL define:

Owner
Publisher
Retention policy
Security classification
Access policy
Replay policy
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL accompany the event throughout its lifecycle.

16. Conformance

A Canonical Event conforms if it:

Inherits the CAO contract.
Implements mandatory event properties.
Preserves immutability.
Supports replay.
Maintains provenance.
Supports event ordering.
Maintains governance metadata.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Distributed event federation.
Cross-organizational event exchange.
Semantic event routing.
Event prioritization.
Digital Twin event streams.
AI-generated engineering events.

Such extensions SHALL preserve canonical event semantics.

